In this lesson, we'll look at the elimination method for solving simultaneous equations. The idea is simple: when you have two equations, you add or subtract them to wipe out one of the variables, leaving you with a single equation you can solve. First, take a look at the form of your equations. The elimination method works best when they're written in standard form, where each side has an x term, a y term, and a constant. Next, if the coefficients of one variable don't already match, you can multiply one or both equations by a clever number so that one variable has matching coefficients in both equations. Then, decide whether to add or subtract. If the matching coefficients have opposite signs, you add the equations and that variable cancels out. If they have the same sign, you subtract instead. Once one variable is gone, solve the simple equation that's left. Finally, take that answer and plug it back into one of the original equations to find the other variable. Now let's see it in action.